# PHP / Laravel testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' testing rules to Laravel's stack: Pest (default for new apps) or PHPUnit, plus Laravel's HTTP/database helpers. Applies only to `*.php` files.

## Layout and naming

- Put framework-touching tests (HTTP, database, queue, auth) in `tests/Feature` and isolated class tests in `tests/Unit`; match the project's existing split.
- Pest: `test('...', function () {...})`/`it(...)`, optionally grouped with `describe(...)`. PHPUnit: extend `Tests\TestCase`, with `public function test_...(): void` or `#[Test]`.
- Name a test for the behavior it verifies ("rejects an order with no line items"), not the method under test.

## Database and factories

- Build test data with model factories (`User::factory()->create([...])`), overriding only the fields the test cares about.
- Apply `RefreshDatabase` (or `DatabaseTransactions` on a project already using it) on any test touching the database; do not rely on execution order.

## HTTP and feature tests

- Exercise routes through `$this->get(...)`, `$this->postJson(...)`, `actingAs($user)` and assert with `assertStatus`, `assertJson`, `assertSee`, `assertRedirect`, not by calling controller methods directly.
- Assert authorization/validation failures explicitly (`assertForbidden()`, `assertInvalid(['field'])`).

## Fakes over real side effects

- Use `Queue::fake()`, `Mail::fake()`, `Event::fake()`, `Storage::fake('disk')`, `Http::fake([...])`; never send a real email, hit a real queue or make a real outbound HTTP request.
- After `Queue::fake()`, assert with `Queue::assertPushed(JobClass::class, fn ($job) => ...)` to check the job's state.

## Assertions and determinism

- Never synchronize with `sleep()`/a fixed delay; use `Queue::fake()` and assert what was dispatched, or run the job inline (`Bus::fake()`/direct `handle()`).
- Prefer Pest's `expect($value)->toBe(...)` (or PHPUnit's `assertSame`) over loose equality when type matters (IDs, enum cases, money values).

## Static analysis and running

- Run the configured static analysis (`./vendor/bin/phpstan analyse`, typically via Larastan) alongside the suite.
- Run `php artisan test`, `./vendor/bin/pest` or `./vendor/bin/phpunit`; do not skip a slow test file without saying so in the report.
